<p>Artemis II astronauts are expected to reach the far side of the moon on Monday as Orion continues a lunar flyby that will take humans farther from Earth than ever before. Nasa says the mission has remained on track since launch on Wednesday. The flight is the first crewed lunar mission since 1972, marking the return of direct human travel to lunar distance after the Apollo era.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/-QXVdeAGLgbH0qUpNCITRspJDYO5WhxKy7-fl3-gwqi2NOjiwZnTOWgq7JjciHFs9TcUkGeUSIiunM2uY07OdX4HY9oO9xBgQBx_kqKVmrBTk8j6Q2bCn1CqKapgosZKTOIB1-SgMfsZIkzyPWoWEJItDn-h-PgWtRRslnRTtL3b1V8prg' alt='This handout picture by an Artemis II crew member provided by NASA shows a sliver of Earth illuminated against the blackness of space through the window of the Orion spacecraft, April 4, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Fifth-day timing: Coverage in Singapore and France noted that the moon encounter comes on the mission’s fifth day, just a few days after launch.</li><li>Flyby profile: Artemis II will not enter lunar orbit; Orion is scheduled to swing around the moon and then begin the trip back to Earth.</li><li>Lunar entry: Le Monde reported that the crew was due to enter the moon’s &#x27;lunar sphere of influence&#x27; overnight on Sunday, a key navigation phase before the flyby.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Nasa: The agency has said Artemis II has been progressing as planned since launch, projecting confidence as the flight heads into its closest pass by the moon. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/apr/05/artemis-ii-astronauts-nasa-far-side-of-moon'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>BBC reporting: Coverage emphasized that the pictures sent back from Artemis II resonate beyond research, inviting audiences to see deep-space photography as both scientific documentation and something personally moving. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clye6j0g840o'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Le Monde: French coverage highlighted the coming entry into the moon’s &#x27;lunar sphere of influence,&#x27; framing the flyby as a moment of precision navigation as much as symbolic progress. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/science/article/2026/04/05/artemis-ii-astronauts-begin-fifth-day-on-historic-moon-mission_6752123_11.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/science/2026/apr/05/artemis-ii-astronauts-nasa-far-side-of-moon'>Artemis II astronauts expected to reach far side of moon on Monday</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/clye6j0g840o'>Artemis&#x27;s stunning Moon pictures - science or holiday photos?</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/what-to-know-about-the-artemis-ii-missions-moon-flyby'>What to know about the Artemis II mission’s Moon flyby</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/science/article/2026/04/05/artemis-ii-astronauts-begin-fifth-day-on-historic-moon-mission_6752123_11.html'>Artemis II astronauts ready for Moon flyby on fifth day of historic mission</a> - lemonde.fr</li></ul>